+  <section class="container">
+    <div class="journal">
+      <h2>Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <div class="journal-text" id="journal-text">
+        <p>Every bag we roast begins months before it reaches our shop, usually with a long conversation over a
+          crackling video call. Our head roaster spends the late winter cupping samples from small cooperatives in
+          Colombia, Ethiopia, and Guatemala, tasting dozens of lots blind and scoring them for sweetness, acidity,
+          body, and finish. Only a handful make the cut, and we never buy a lot we haven't tasted ourselves.</p>
+        <p>We buy directly from producers whenever we can, paying a premium that is typically thirty to fifty percent
+          above the Fair Trade minimum. Direct relationships let us agree on prices before the harvest, so farmers can
+          invest in better drying beds, shade trees, and wet mills without worrying about a collapse in the market. It
+          also means we know the names of the families who grew our coffee, and we print them on our bags.</p>
+        <p>When green coffee arrives at our warehouse, we test a sample roast of every shipment against the original
+          cupping notes. If a lot has faded in transit or doesn't match what we tasted at origin, we send it back.
+          Approved beans rest for a few weeks, then we roast in small batches of no more than thirty pounds so we can
+          adjust the profile for each harvest's density and moisture.</p>
+        <p>Finally, we track what we learn. Each season we visit at least two farms, bring back feedback from our
+          customers, and share what sold well and what didn't. Sourcing, for us, is less a transaction than a
+          year-round partnership, and the best cup of the day is the one that honors everyone who made it possible.</p>
+      </div>
+      <button type="button" class="btn" id="summarize-btn">Summarize</button>
+      <div class="summary" id="summary" aria-live="polite" hidden></div>
+    </div>
+  </section>
+
